WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
Begin by separating the information available to the learner.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:08.490
In this lecture, lipreading uses visible speech, facial expression, and body gestures.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.120
Speechreading adds the auditory signal.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.730
The deck notes that these terms are sometimes used interchangeably.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.520
Here, keep its distinction clear: watching without hearing and watching while using

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.780
available hearing are different input conditions.

00:00:22.835 --> 00:00:24.935
Now focus on what the face can reveal.

00:00:25.485 --> 00:00:29.725
Front-of-mouth gestures and larger lip, jaw, and tongue movements are easier to

00:00:29.725 --> 00:00:30.025
see.

00:00:30.065 --> 00:00:33.935
But a visible movement does not uniquely identify a sound.

00:00:34.735 --> 00:00:38.575
The sounds represented by B, M, and P all involve lip closure.

00:00:39.125 --> 00:00:43.305
The same visible closure can therefore leave more than one possible sound.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.540
A viseme groups speech sounds with similar facial appearance.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.820
A homophene concerns whole words that sound different but look alike on

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:52.240
the lips.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.360
The deck gives rise and rice as an example.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.170
Keep the level straight: a sound group is not a word group.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:03.830
Available hearing and sentence context may help resolve an ambiguity that vision

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:04.870
alone leaves open.

00:01:05.875 --> 00:01:10.195
The appearance also changes across surrounding sounds and across talkers.

00:01:10.745 --> 00:01:15.645
Coarticulation means neighboring sounds influence how a sound is produced and appears.

00:01:16.195 --> 00:01:19.335
The following vowel can change the visible form of a consonant.

00:01:19.885 --> 00:01:21.685
Stress changes the message too.

00:01:22.245 --> 00:01:25.475
Emphasizing YOU in a question directs attention toward who acted.

00:01:25.855 --> 00:01:28.595
These are reasons not to treat a mouth shape as a fixed

00:01:28.595 --> 00:01:29.555
letter on a screen.

00:01:30.495 --> 00:01:31.995
Speech rate adds another demand.

00:01:32.545 --> 00:01:35.458
The deck gives a typical range of one hundred fifty to two

00:01:35.458 --> 00:01:37.075
hundred fifty words per minute.

00:01:37.475 --> 00:01:40.515
Emotion, fatigue, and speaking style can change that rate.

00:01:41.065 --> 00:01:44.525
More time can help a learner inspect the available cues, but slowing

00:01:44.525 --> 00:01:49.165
down does not create a missing sound distinction or justify exaggerated movements.

00:01:50.245 --> 00:01:52.525
Finally, keep the source limits visible.

00:01:53.075 --> 00:01:57.295
The percentages in the slides describe estimates of visible speech, not an

00:01:57.295 --> 00:01:59.335
individual word-recognition score.

00:02:00.135 --> 00:02:03.895
The deck also uses conflicting language about intelligence and performance.

00:02:04.445 --> 00:02:08.485
We preserve that uncertainty instead of turning it into a universal rule.

00:02:09.035 --> 00:02:13.055
The central checkpoint is to ask what information is actually available, and

00:02:13.055 --> 00:02:14.455
what ambiguity remains.

